{
  "title": "Unpacking DPO and PPO: Disentangling Best Practices for Learning from Preference Feedback",
  "authors": [
    "Hamish Ivison",
    "Yizhong Wang",
    "Jiacheng Liu",
    "Zeqiu Wu",
    "Valentina Pyatkin",
    "Nathan Lambert",
    "Noah A. Smith",
    "Yejin Choi",
    "Hannaneh Hajishirzi"
  ],
  "year": 2024,
  "arxivId": "2406.09279",
  "abstract": "Learning from preference feedback is a crucial step in improving language models. This work systematically investigates the core components of learning from preference feedback, including preference data, learning algorithm, reward model, and policy training prompts. The authors find that using synthetic preference datasets and training with PPO and a large reward model performs best overall. They also suggest that targeted prompts can improve performance when coupled with powerful reward models.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "Learning from preference feedback involves training a model to generate responses based on human preferences. The two most popular algorithms for this are PPO and DPO, which differ in their approach to learning from preferences. <strong>PPO trains a reward model on preference data and then uses it to score model-generated responses</strong>, while <strong>DPO directly trains the policy model on preference data</strong>.",
      "visualization": {
        "type": "flow-diagram",
